WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.419 --> 00:00:18.509
Doctor: "Well,

00:00:18.509 --> 00:00:20.430
what do we have here?"

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.760
Mother: "He burned his hand on a stove."

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.679
Doctor: "Did you warn him not to touch a hot stove?"

00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:29.269
Mother "Of course I did!"
Doctor: "Well,

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:31.390
I'm afraid we can't help him. Clearly,

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:35.350
he did this to himself."

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:38.580
That's absurd, isn't it?

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.520
That a doctor would compromise care for a
patient because he did it to himself.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.420
But unfortunately overweight and obese people
are the victims of this kind of

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.830
irrational weight prejudice almost
everyday.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:51.600
Whether it's in the

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.850
form of negative attitudes, societal
stigma,

00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:01.850
or unfair treatment.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:30.909
My name is Emme, and as the first plus size supermodel
trust me, I know what it's like to be

00:01:30.909 --> 00:01:32.740
judged based on my size.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:37.980
And that's why I'm here today, to raise
your awareness of exactly what weight bias is,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.210
how it shows up in medical practices,

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.620
and who its victims, sources and
consequences are. I'm hopeful that by the

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.780
time that we come to the end of this
brief journey, you'll be inspired to

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.080
make subtle but critical changes in
your own medical practice and perhaps

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.990
even adjust your own perceptions in
treatment of your overweight and obese patients.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.190
Weight stigma is bias and discrimination
discrimination aimed at overweight

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:02.450
people based

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.470
on a series of social attitudes that
people develop that can start very early

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:11.000
in life that assume that there's
something wrong with overweight people

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:18.000
and that they should be punished for
their condition.

00:02:25.259 --> 00:02:26.449
Patient: "Hi, could you help me?"

00:02:26.449 --> 00:02:28.539
Receptionist: "Do you have an appointment?"
Patient: "Yes, I made it

00:02:28.539 --> 00:02:33.069
a year ago."
Receptionist: "Name?" 
Patient: "Cole."

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:35.110
Receptionist: "Natasha?"

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.999
"You can take a seat."

00:02:38.999 --> 00:02:44.930
"Over a year ago? She must really hate
coming to the doctor."

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:49.419
One important area were wight bias is
expressed is in medical care.

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:53.099
Overweight people are very often
reluctant to go get medical care,

00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:56.349
preventive services in particular,
because of

00:02:56.349 --> 00:02:59.790
biased attitudes they feel that they're
going to encounter at the hands of

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.120
health care professionals. There've been
studies with physicians with medical

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.790
students, with nurses, with dietitians, with
psychologist and with other health care

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:09.729
professionals,

00:03:09.729 --> 00:03:13.129
showing quite negative attitudes and
feelings that there's something wrong

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:14.790
with the overweight person

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:18.549
that their weight condition is their
fault and that because they haven't lost

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:25.549
weight there's something seriously
lacking in their personality

00:03:25.999 --> 00:03:27.039
Child Patient: "Mom, mom. That lady's huge."

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.380
Mother: "Shhh."

00:03:39.639 --> 00:03:43.569
Weight bias can be as hurtful and
difficult to counter as a racial

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:44.339
prejudice,

00:03:44.339 --> 00:03:47.859
and yet there's no anti-discrimination
laws to protect people who are

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:48.939
overweight.

00:03:48.939 --> 00:03:53.079
Weight bias is so prevalent in our
society that most people don't even

00:03:53.079 --> 00:03:54.659
stop to question it.

00:03:54.659 --> 00:03:58.879
As a result, overweight individuals
confront negative stereotypes and

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:02.169
prejudice often on a daily basis

00:04:02.169 --> 00:04:06.150
It's important to recognize just how common
this problem is in the United States.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:09.489
We recently examined a nationally
representative sample of American adults

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:13.299
and found out the prevalence of weight
discrimination is comparable to racial

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:17.669
discrimination, and is in fact more common than
racial discrimination among women.

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:21.379
We also found that the prevalence of weight
discrimination is more common than

00:04:21.379 --> 00:04:26.969
discrimination due to ethnicity, religion,
sexual orientation or physical disability.

00:04:26.969 --> 00:04:31.489
It's important to consider that
statistically six out of ten patients in

00:04:31.489 --> 00:04:32.559
your waiting room

00:04:32.559 --> 00:04:35.229
are already overweight or obese,

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:39.849
and that number is rapidly on the rise.
And most of you know that obesity has

00:04:39.849 --> 00:04:43.400
doubled in the past twenty years in both
children and adults,

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.099
and frighteningly,

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:48.259
it's tripled in teens. One in five
children is overweight

00:04:48.259 --> 00:04:52.040
and overweight children tend to become
overweight adults.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.440
Thirty percent of adults are obese

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.840
and we're right on track for this trend to
continue.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.990
Whether or not you want to deal with this
the obesity epidemic is increasingly

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.919
going to impact your practice and as
these numbers continue to grow anything

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.919
you can do to make your overweight and
obese patients feel more comfortable

00:05:10.919 --> 00:05:12.129
and accepted

00:05:12.129 --> 00:05:16.860
will translate into an improved working
environment and a more successful practice

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.360
and most importantly,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.800
better health outcomes for your patients.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.550
Receptionist: "Miss Cole,"

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.520
"The nurse will take you in."

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.990
The stigma of obesity is so strong,
that even health-care professionals

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:31.900
specializing in the treatment of obesity

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.469
hold negative attitudes towards the
obese and

00:05:34.469 --> 00:05:37.600
infer that these people have blameworthy
behavior

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.819
Nurse: "Step on the scale please."

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:40.629
Patient: "I'd rather not".

00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:44.830
Nurse: "We have to weigh you."
Patient: "It's the same as last time."

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:51.830
Nurse: "We need it for our medical records."

00:05:56.359 --> 00:05:59.279
Nurse: "Two hundred and sixty pounds."

00:05:59.279 --> 00:06:01.509
"What, are you five four?"

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:03.479
"You've gained weight since last year."

00:06:03.479 --> 00:06:06.519
"It looks like your diet isn't working."

00:06:06.519 --> 00:06:11.839
"Please move into the examination room. The
doctor will be right with you."

00:06:11.839 --> 00:06:15.169
Nurse: "How hard is it to push yourself away
from the table?"

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:17.500
Receptionist: "I know, and get off the couch."

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:24.110
"Nothin' to it, but to do it."
Nurse: "You have to work hard to get that overweight."

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:28.129
Physicians limit their interactions with
obese patients, are ambivalent about

00:06:28.129 --> 00:06:32.349
treatment roles and are reluctant to
perform certain screenings.

00:06:32.349 --> 00:06:36.989
Studies have revealed that nurses too
view obese patients as lazy, over-

00:06:36.989 --> 00:06:40.969
indulgent, non-compliant and less
successful than their average weight

00:06:40.969 --> 00:06:42.369
counterparts.

00:06:42.369 --> 00:06:47.709
Nurses self-reported alarming statistics.
One study found that in a third to half of

00:06:47.709 --> 00:06:49.719
the nurses surveyed admitted

00:06:49.719 --> 00:06:52.869
feeling uncomfortable caring for obese
patients.

00:06:52.869 --> 00:06:56.199
Many would prefer not to touch
obese patients.

00:06:56.199 --> 00:07:00.289
A quarter of them agree that obese
patients repulse them and fully a

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:04.630
third we prefer not to care for obese
patients at all.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.889
And these are their caregivers.

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:14.889
Nurse: "This thing isn't big enough for you."

00:07:15.749 --> 00:07:19.120
Nurse: "Here, change into this."

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:26.120
Nurse (out the door): "Does anyone know where the 
extra large blood pressure cuff is?"

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:37.110
Weight bias could affect an individual's
health in many ways.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:40.389
just being a victim of discrimination
could affect your health but then you

00:07:40.389 --> 00:07:45.639
add to that the fact that people don't
get care as they should because of the biased

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:48.149
attitudes they expect to encounter.

00:07:48.149 --> 00:07:50.379
It creates a real problem.

00:07:50.379 --> 00:07:54.419
health care providers are not immune to
wait bias and unfortunately many overweight

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:57.749
and obese patients report
negative experiences from multiple

00:07:57.749 --> 00:07:59.080
groups of providers.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.389
As an example, we surveyed over twenty
four hundred overweight and obese adults and

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:06.710
we asked them to tell us about the
different sources of weight bias in their

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:07.639
lives.

00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:11.759
What we found is that physicians are the
second most common source of weight bias

00:08:11.759 --> 00:08:12.499
reported.

00:08:12.499 --> 00:08:17.059
Fully sixty nine percent of people
reported weight bias from physicians,

00:08:17.059 --> 00:08:20.939
Forty six percent from nurses, thirty seven
percent from dietitians and twenty one

00:08:20.939 --> 00:08:24.729
percent for mental health professionals.
So this really is a common experience

00:08:24.729 --> 00:08:28.109
for many individuals in the health-care
environment.

00:08:28.109 --> 00:08:28.909
Let's take a look

00:08:28.909 --> 00:08:30.260
at the impact

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.079
of all of this to see why this is such a
critical

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:35.060
and urgent issue.

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:38.810
Well there are a range of negative
consequences of weight bias for both

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:40.980
emotional and physical health.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:44.630
For example we know that individuals who
experience weight bias are more

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:50.230
vulnerable to depression, low self-esteem,
poor body image and even suicidal

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:50.960
behaviors.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:54.680
We also know that individuals who
experience weight bias are more likely to

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.080
engage in unhealthy eating behaviors
like binge eating and avoiding physical

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.520
activity, both of which may ultimately
only reinforce weight gain and additional

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.510
obesity.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:08.189
It's not a leap to conclude that when
patients experience bias

00:09:08.189 --> 00:09:11.060
their preventative care is compromised.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:13.980
So what's the solution? What can you do?

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.810
The solutions are so simple.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:20.260
Treat your overweight and obesity
patience with the same courtesy you

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:23.760
treat all of your other patients.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:29.440
Receptionist: "Mrs. Cole, how are you today?
Patient: "Good, thank you."
Receptionist: "Good, are you here for your 10:30 appointment?"
Patient: "I sure am."

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.390
Receptionist: "Great, just take a seat. The doctor will
see you shortly."
Patient: "Thank you."

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.010
There are a number of things that

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:39.360
health professionals and medical offices
can do to combat weight bias.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.080
The first is just to take a look at the
physical environment of the office. So

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.700
for example, making sure that in the
waiting room there's room for people to

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:50.060
move around, that there are
chairs that are large enough to

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.660
accommodate all patients.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.090
And it's important to even look at the
reading material in the office because

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:57.199
things like,

00:09:57.199 --> 00:09:58.740
you know, fashion magazines

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:03.940
that have a lot of very stereotypical
images of women and exaggerated images

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:08.270
of, you know, female beauty can sometimes
be disturbing to patients feeling like

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:12.070
they're constantly surrounded by those
images.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:16.090
Another thing the medical professionals
can do is make sure that the place where

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:21.060
they weigh patients is private, so we
recommend that the scale is often a

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:25.500
separate section of the office, it's not
visible to other people.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.490
And another thing that helps a lot is to
actually ask the patient if they would

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:33.460
like to be weighed. It's a way of showing
respect for the patient's decision

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.790
whether or not they feel that that's
important for them to do during that

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:38.390
visit.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:39.210
Nurse: "Hello,

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:40.900
would you like to be weighed today?"

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.379
Patient: "No, there haven't been any significant
changes."

00:10:43.379 --> 00:10:44.180
Nurse: "Fair enough.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:49.490
There's a gown right there and I'll be right
back to take your blood pressure."
Patient: "Great."

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:51.699
Provide larger sized gowns,

00:10:51.699 --> 00:10:54.860
larger, sturdier examination tables.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:11:00.440
Provide larger blood pressure cuffs to
your patients who require a larger size.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:04.480
It's very important for health care
providers to increase awareness about

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.510
their own attitudes and assumptions based
on weight

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:11.110
and providers can ask themselves
questions like, Do i make assumptions

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:16.330
based on weight regarding a person's
character or intelligence or success or

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.380
lifestyle?

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:22.310
Am I comfortable working with patients of
all sizes?

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:26.800
Do i get appropriate feedback to
encourage healthful behavior change?

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.990
Am I sensitive to the needs and
concerns of obese individuals?

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:34.540
Do I treat the individual or only the
condition?

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:39.030
Another important aspect of reducing weight
bias to really understand the

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:40.720
complexity of obesity.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:44.660
People think that for someone who's
overweight all they need to lose weight

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:50.330
is some self control and trying harder
to eat less and exercise more.

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:54.350
If that only worked we wouldn't have the
problem that we have today. The causes of

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:59.380
obesity are very complex. It is a
combination of genetic factors,

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:03.770
environmental factors, psychological
factors and it's important to recognize

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:09.010
that so that when we look at someone
and we see whatever their body weight is we

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:16.010
don't make assumptions about their
behaviors based on that information.

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:20.360
It's important for providers to
recognize that patients may have had

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:24.510
negative experiences with weight bias from
previous providers so when they come

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:30.570
into the office for the first time
providers need to be aware of these issues.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.970
We often hear from patients that when
they go into the doctor for a problem

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:38.660
such as an ear ache that the doctor
frequently will blame the issue on their

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:44.820
body weight even though it has nothing
to do with the presenting problem.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:48.620
Many patients have tried to lose weight
repeatedly in the past but have been

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:50.240
unsuccessful,

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:54.050
And so when a patient comes into the
office for the first time, telling a patient

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.490
that they need to lose weight

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:58.580
is something that the patient has
probably heard many times before and

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:05.150
it's not necessarily the most helpful
advice to give.

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:09.570
One thing that providers can do is to
really emphasize behavior changes rather

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:13.330
than just a number on the scale. We
want to be setting goals for patients

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:15.440
that are realistic and achievable.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:19.360
So things like can they increase their
consumption of fruits and vegetables,

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:21.350
reduce intake of soda,

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.430
walk more frequently during the day,

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:30.430
very tangible strategies and goals
that patients can work on to improve their health.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:35.180
It's also important for providers to
recognize that relatively small weight

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:36.610
losses can have

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.890
important improvements for overall
health and so even if an individual

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:43.730
is able to lose five or ten percent of 
their body weight,

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:47.170
that can have very important implications for their
overall health.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:50.750
They may be able to get off certain medications or
lower their blood pressure, and

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:54.070
communicating this to patients can be
very empowering as they try to make behavior

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:57.790
changes.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:02.110
If weight loss was easy, we would not have the
current obesity epidemic that we have

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:06.300
so it's very important for providers to
acknowledge how difficult this is

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:08.610
and to provide support.

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:12.670
Patients are in three times more likely
to address diet and lifestyle changes

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:17.100
if their doctor constructively and
sensitively called their way to their

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:18.280
attention.

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:22.520
Yet less than half of physicians
actually do.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.500
Doctor: "Well, Mrs. Cole, all your vitals look
great,

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:27.230
it sounds like you're doing great.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:29.950
Would you mind if we talked about your
weight?"
Patient: "Sure.

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:33.070
I know I could eat better, get more exercise."

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:35.810
Doctor: "I'm glad to hear you're thinking about ways to
improve your health

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:39.710
but it's important to remember that body
weight is only partly determined by diet

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:42.070
and exercise.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:45.070
Still, we can all stand to make lifestyle
improvements.

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:49.320
Let's talk about what you're doing now
and how effective that is."
Patient: "Okay."

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:54.280
The medical community offers great
opportunities to help address the issue of weight bias,

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:58.450
because with positive attitudes, people
will get better care but not only that but

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:02.690
it'll be modeling for the world
about how overweight people should be

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:07.050
treated what weight is. It shows that weight
is an issue that needs to be

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:11.320
dealt with in medical settings in a
straightforward, positive, constructive

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:12.240
way.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:16.170
And as people see this then they
see that weight isn't an issue

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.360
where there should be tremendous
prejudice and stigma

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:23.320
directed at individuals, rather it should be
a straightforward medical condition just

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:25.230
like anything else.

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:29.710
In spite of millions of dollars spent
every year on weight loss efforts,

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:31.570
long-term results in America

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:35.580
point to the fact that they are
obviously other factors that play beyond

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:37.410
intention and commitment.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:41.900
Yet the increasing prevalence of obesity
in our society has oddly not reduced

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:46.430
weight bias. With more and more
people overweight, one might think

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:47.960
tolerance would increase,

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:49.180
but it hasn't.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:53.330
At the Rudd Center, we work hard to
eliminate the problem of weight bias.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:57.660
We believe that there's a strong social
injustice occurring here,

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.090
that hundreds of millions of people
around the world are deeply affected by

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:06.300
this and it just doesn't need to occur.
Race bias doesn't need to occur, gender

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:10.910
bias doesn't need to occur and weight
bias doesn't need to occur.

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:14.650
Thank you for taking the time to watch
this educational video on weight

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:15.360
stigma.

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:19.720
For more information please visit the
Rudd Center for Food Policy and Obesity

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:21.510
at yaleruddcenter.org.

